## Chapter Six: The Gap

The compliance clock expired at noon.

B noticed at eleven fifty-three and spent the intervening seven minutes finishing a system review that had nothing to do with the apartment anomaly, which was either professional discipline or motivated distraction and he had decided not to adjudicate.

The anomaly report opened on his desk glass.

It was a standard form. Origin trace. Duration. Classification attempt. Recommended action.

A footer pulsed beneath the empty fields.

PRIVATE REMEMBERING HAS INSUFFICIENT WITNESS.

UNSHARED BEFORE MAY ENTER MATERIAL CONDITION.

B dismissed it without reading it twice.

He filled in the fields he could fill.

The field he could not fill was the one that said: *Provenance reference.*

A provenance reference told Asterion where the anomaly had come from — which correction event had displaced the pressure, which domain had filed it, which case number linked the residue to its source. Without a provenance reference the report was a record of a symptom with no cause attached, which the system would accept and archive and take no action on, which was technically compliant and professionally inadequate.

"Mote," he said. "The apartment anomaly. Origin trace. What can you access?"

"Standard range complete. No correction event in the residential zone within the standard lookback window."

"Extend the window."

"Extended. One gap in Asterion's public correction registry. Residential continuity domain. Administrative district five. The event record is sealed for continuity reasons. I can provide a filing reference but not content access."

B looked at the provenance reference field.

A sealed event record was not unusual. Asterion sealed records for operational reasons, legal reasons, public health reasons, and a category it described as "stability maintenance," which covered everything the other categories were too specific to contain. He had worked around sealed records dozens of times.

What was unusual was that the gap existed at all.

The public correction registry was a summary record. It did not contain sensitive details. It contained: date, domain, classification category, case status. Sealed records appeared in the registry with their metadata intact and their content behind an access restriction. You could see the shape of the event even if you could not see inside it.

This gap had no shape.

The date was there. Administrative district five. Residential continuity domain.

Everything else was absent.

Not restricted. Absent. The field that should have held a classification category contained a reference code he had never seen in twelve years of professional access.

He looked at it for a moment.

"Mote. That reference code."

"Yes."

"What is it?"

A pause that was slightly too long.

"A continuity exception designation. Applied when the corrected record itself would constitute a continuity risk if partially visible."

"Meaning the shape of the event is enough to cause harm."

"That is one interpretation."

"What's another?"

Mote was quiet.

"What's another interpretation," B said again.

"The classification was applied to prevent pattern recognition across records that individually appear unrelated."

B looked at the gap.

"When was this sealed?"

"Five years ago. The sealing classification was applied two weeks after the event date."

Five years ago.

He was already doing the arithmetic before he decided to.

He stopped himself.

This was paperwork. He was doing paperwork. He needed a provenance reference. The reference pointed to a sealed record. He could not access the sealed record through the public registry. He could request access through proper channels, wait six to twelve weeks, and receive either a redacted summary or a denial.

He filed that as the next step and closed the anomaly report.

He opened it again four seconds later.

"Is there a researcher credential on the sealed record?" he asked.

"The researcher field is also absent from the public registry entry."

"But there is a researcher."

"Most correction events have associated personnel records."

"Can you access the personnel index for district five, five years ago, residential continuity domain, without querying the sealed record directly?"

A longer pause.

"Yes."

"Do it."

The pause extended.

"Mote."

"I am determining whether the personnel index is within the scope of your current access credentials."

"Is it?"

"Technically."

"Then do it."

The glass populated with a partial result. Most fields were standard personnel classification data — role categories, department identifiers, credential levels. The name field was redacted. The credential identifier was a number he did not recognize.

And then, in the associated authorization field, in a sub-entry that should not have been there at all:

His own credential number.

B looked at it.

"Mote."

"Yes."

"My credential is listed in the authorization field of the personnel record attached to the sealed correction event."

"Yes."

"Why."

It was not a question. It came out flat, the way questions come out when the available answers are all bad.

"I cannot access the content of the sealed record."

"I know. I'm asking you what you know."

"I know that your credential appears in the authorization field."

"That means I was present. Or I authorized something. Or I was the subject."

Mote did not answer.

"Which one."

"I cannot provide the relevant record."

B pushed back from the desk.

The apartment's grief had been returning to the workroom since the recreational dive. He had noticed it every morning — the pressure settling near the Chrysanthemum, pausing near his chair, then withdrawing. He had been noting it without examining it, which was the professional version of not wanting to know.

He looked at the Chrysanthemum now.

"Is there anything in the public Asterion archive that mentions my name in connection with displaced-correction research?"

A very long pause.

"There is a sealed technical paper. The metadata visible in the public index lists two researchers. One is redacted. One is you."

B went still.

"I wrote a paper on displaced-correction patterns."

"A paper exists with your name listed as researcher."

"That is not what I said."

"No."

"I have no memory of writing it."

"No."

The word arrived without elaboration.

The room was quiet. Through the window, Meridian was doing its mid-afternoon calibration, adjusting light frequencies toward the productivity band, rerouting pedestrian traffic away from a construction zone that had been creating what the transit advisory described as "spatial disorientation events."

The apartment's wellness panel offered a rest recommendation. Biometric threshold exceeded. Suggested options, helpfully listed: a twenty-minute rest, a walk along the lower promenade, connection to a licensed support service.

Beneath the options, in smaller type:

YOUR CERTAINTY DOES NOT SUPPORT FLOOR.

PLEASE RETURN TO APPROVED HOME SHAPE.

B looked at the notification.

He could not determine whether the system was responding to his cortisol level or to the search pattern he had been running across sealed Asterion records. The Choir did not distinguish between those two categories. Disruption registered as disruption. The Float maintaining its agreed-upon version of the day.

B had built his professional identity around being difficult to deceive. He knew how corrections felt from inside the field. He knew the signatures of institutional memory management, the specific quality of records that had been adjusted rather than invented, the way certain seams sat at angles that looked natural until you checked them against adjacent structures.

He had never checked himself.

The thought was not comfortable.

"The paper," he said. "What's the title?"

"The title field is also sealed."

"The abstract?"

"Sealed."

"The journal?"

"It was not submitted to an external journal. It is an internal Asterion document."

"Filed under which division?"

A pause.

"Aletheia."

B looked at the empty provenance reference field on his anomaly report.


He was not going to do this correctly by pulling records.

He understood that now.

The records were the wrong instrument for this problem. The records had been arranged to prevent exactly the kind of cross-domain pattern recognition he was attempting. Every time he pulled one, he was working inside the Choir's filing system, which was the system that had produced the problem.

He needed to go under.

Not recreationally this time.

With a specific objective. With a boundary. With the understanding that whatever he found was going to require him to act differently afterward, because you could not go looking for evidence of your own correction and come back from it the same person you were before.

"Mote," he said.

"Yes."

"I need to enter the Foam. Not exploratory. I need to trace my own authorization history past the sealed event."

A pause.

"That is not a standard use of the Chrysanthemum."

"I know."

"Your chemical state is not optimal for deep emergence."

"What is it?"

Mote brought up the current profile.

STIMULANT RESIDUE: TRACE
NUTRITIONAL DEFICIT: MODERATE
SLEEP DEBT: SIGNIFICANT
ELEVATED CORTISOL: CONSISTENT WITH ADMINISTRATIVE DISTRESS

"Define a return condition," Mote said.

B looked at the gap in the public correction registry.

The absent shape. Five years ago. His own credential. A paper he didn't remember writing.

"If I find what I'm looking for," he said, "I surface immediately and we discuss what to do next."

"That is not a return condition. That is a contingency."

"What's the difference?"

"A return condition specifies when you come back regardless of whether you have found what you are looking for."

B rubbed his face.

"Seven minutes," he said. "Automatic taper at the first loss of identity continuity."

"Agreed."

Mote did not say anything else.

B looked at the Chrysanthemum.

The grief in the ducts had settled near it again, the way it did now, waiting.

He fitted the contact band around the base of his skull.

"If something changes," he said. "If the anomaly does something unexpected. Pull me out."

"Yes."

"Without asking."

"Yes."

B closed his eyes.

The Chrysanthemum opened.
